# Integration Boundaries

See [ARCHITECTURE.md](../ARCHITECTURE.md) for the crate map and the storage,
schema, UI, and inference design. The graph facade is synchronous and usable
without a server: do not give `KnowledgeGraph` an `InferenceQueue` or a
provider. Callers opt into AI separately.

## Wiring a capability

`AppView::do_init_lemonade()` in
[app_view/lemonade.rs](../crates/u-forge/src/app_view/lemonade.rs) is the
desktop example. Follow the same discovery and activation boundaries:

1. Share one `Arc<LemonadeConnection>` for the normalized API URL, ownership,
   and separate inference and admin credentials. See
   [runtime selection](environment-config.mdc#lemonade-connection-selection)
   for the distinction between desktop startup and loopback probing.
2. Use `LemonadeServerCatalog::discover_with_connection` before selecting
   capabilities. `/models` is required; `/system-info` and `/health` enrich
   the snapshot independently and retain failures in `CatalogDiagnostics`.
   Do not infer installed models, backends, or device presence from defaults.
3. Build a `ModelSelector` from the snapshot and app configuration to select
   downloaded models per capability and device slot.
4. Implement the capability trait and construct providers through
   `ProviderFactory::build_with_connection`. The factory handles GPU
   contention and coordinated chat runtime profiles. Catalog-loaded model
   IDs are warm-start hints; reuse requires a live profile check at lease
   acquisition. Do not add a separate device worker layer.
5. Register the built provider with `InferenceQueueBuilder::with_provider`.
   Keep HTTP details out of business logic. Missing inference should disable
   only the affected capabilities, not graph operations.

See [Rust patterns](rust-patterns.mdc) for the provider split, cancellation
ownership, and typed error boundaries. See
[testing and debugging](testing-debugging.mdc) for live-provider fixtures.
